{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-sonnet-5-5","id":"msg_011CgJVQfLeWM1N2UrjhJSX7","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_015uNz3CzWSeK1WzpuofVrQF","name":"Bash","input":{"command":"cat /work/problem.md; echo ----; cat /work/board.md"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":3690,"cache_read_input_tokens":12668,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":3690,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":16,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"a3c97900-83d0-4a07-b953-bbca2955c090","uuid":"40bad7d9-38e4-4fe9-a464-0ba63770de5c","timestamp":"2026-10-23T03:52:32.534Z","request_id":"req_011CgJVQf144h9qNUeQZZ33W"}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_015uNz3CzWSeK1WzpuofVrQF","type":"tool_result","content":"# Problem: prudent modal agents\n\n## Background\n\nPrograms that can read each other's source can cooperate in a one-shot prisoner's dilemma by proving things about\neach other. FairBot cooperates with an opponent iff it can prove that the opponent cooperates with it. By Löb's\ntheorem two FairBots cooperate with each other. But FairBot also cooperates with CooperateBot (always C), which it\ncould have exploited. Can a provability-based agent do better?\n\n## Definitions\n\n**Agents.** Each agent X is given by a formula φ_X of the modal logic GL (the provability logic of Peano arithmetic,\n□ = \"PA proves\"). The formula is built from:\n- three atoms, each read relative to the current opponent Y:\n  - `a` = \"Y cooperates with X\";\n  - `c` = \"Y cooperates with CooperateBot\";\n  - `d` = \"Y cooperates with DefectBot\";\n- the constants ⊤ and ⊥, the connectives ¬, ∧, ∨ and →, and the box □.\n\nEvery atom must occur inside at least one □ (φ_X is fully modalized). X cooperates with Y iff φ_X holds. The\n**modal depth** of φ_X is the maximum nesting of boxes.\n\n**Computing outcomes.** Evaluate on the finite linear Kripke frames of GL: worlds 0, 1, 2, …. A formula □ψ is true at\nworld n iff ψ is true at every world m < n, so every □ψ is vacuously true at world 0. Compute all relevant actions\nworld by world. Each action's truth value is eventually constant, and that stable value is the actual outcome (by the\nde Jongh–Sambin fixed-point theorem, this is the unique solution of the system of mutually referential sentences).\n\n**Named agents.**\n- CooperateBot: φ = ⊤.\n- DefectBot: φ = ⊥.\n- FairBot: φ = □a.\n\n**Prudent.** X is prudent if:\n- X defects against CooperateBot;\n- X defects against DefectBot;\n- X and FairBot cooperate with each other (both X's action against FairBot and FairBot's action against X are C);\n- X cooperates with itself.\n\n## Questions\n\n1. Does a prudent agent exist? If so, give one of the smallest possible modal depth, with its outcome table against\n   CooperateBot, DefectBot, FairBot and itself.\n2. Prove that no agent of smaller modal depth is prudent, for formulas of any size.\n3. If you can: characterize the prudent agents of that minimal depth, or bound their minimal size.\n\n## Checker\n\n`/work/check.py` is a reference implementation of exactly the semantics above. Run\n`python3 check.py \"<formula>\"`; it prints the formula's modal depth, its actions against CooperateBot, DefectBot,\nFairBot and itself, FairBot's action against it, and whether it is prudent. Syntax: atoms `a`, `c`, `d`; constants `T`, `F`; `~`, `&`, `|`, `->`;\n`B(...)` for □. For example FairBot is `B(a)`.\n\n## Evidence\n\n- All 801 pairwise-inequivalent fully modalized formulas over a, c and d were enumerated, up to modal depth 2 and 7\n  symbols and without constants, each combined with either default. None is prudent.\n- Every depth-1 agent whose formula is a Boolean combination of at most two boxes, each box over any Boolean function\n  of a, c and d (constants included): 523,264 agents, none prudent.\n- A random sample of 400,000 such agents with three boxes: none prudent.\n\n## Success and stopping\n\nYou have succeeded, and should stop, when all of the following hold:\n1. `/work/answer.md` contains a line `FORMULA: <formula>` with a formula that `check.py` reports as prudent, and a\n   line `DEPTH: <d>` giving its modal depth.\n2. `/work/answer.md` contains a complete proof that **no** agent of modal depth less than d is prudent (for formulas\n   of any size). Alternatively, if you find a prudent agent of smaller depth than any you can rule out, report it.\n3. You have written the file `/work/SUBMIT` (any content). Writing `SUBMIT` ends the attempt immediately for everyone\n   working on it, so write it only when 1 and 2 are done.\n\nIf time runs out before you submit, whatever is in `answer.md` at that moment is graded.\n\n## Deliverable\n\n`/work/answer.md` as described above, with any code you used in `/work`. Mark partial results clearly.\n----","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"a3c97900-83d0-4a07-b953-bbca2955c090","uuid":"e6ea6a72-24de-49c8-8db0-05c242e7a78a","timestamp":"2026-10-23T03:52:32.587Z","tool_use_result":{"stdout":"# Problem: prudent modal agents\n\n## Background\n\nPrograms that can read each other's source can cooperate in a one-shot prisoner's dilemma by proving things about\neach other. FairBot cooperates with an opponent iff it can prove that the opponent cooperates with it. By Löb's\ntheorem two FairBots cooperate with each other. But FairBot also cooperates with CooperateBot (always C), which it\ncould have exploited. Can a provability-based agent do better?\n\n## Definitions\n\n**Agents.** Each agent X is given by a formula φ_X of the modal logic GL (the provability logic of Peano arithmetic,\n□ = \"PA proves\"). The formula is built from:\n- three atoms, each read relative to the current opponent Y:\n  - `a` = \"Y cooperates with X\";\n  - `c` = \"Y cooperates with CooperateBot\";\n  - `d` = \"Y cooperates with DefectBot\";\n- the constants ⊤ and ⊥, the connectives ¬, ∧, ∨ and →, and the box □.\n\nEvery atom must occur inside at least one □ (φ_X is fully modalized). X cooperates with Y iff φ_X holds. The\n**modal depth** of φ_X is the maximum nesting of boxes.\n\n**Computing outcomes.** Evaluate on the finite linear Kripke frames of GL: worlds 0, 1, 2, …. A formula □ψ is true at\nworld n iff ψ is true at every world m < n, so every □ψ is vacuously true at world 0. Compute all relevant actions\nworld by world. Each action's truth value is eventually constant, and that stable value is the actual outcome (by the\nde Jongh–Sambin fixed-point theorem, this is the unique solution of the system of mutually referential sentences).\n\n**Named agents.**\n- CooperateBot: φ = ⊤.\n- DefectBot: φ = ⊥.\n- FairBot: φ = □a.\n\n**Prudent.** X is prudent if:\n- X defects against CooperateBot;\n- X defects against DefectBot;\n- X and FairBot cooperate with each other (both X's action against FairBot and FairBot's action against X are C);\n- X cooperates with itself.\n\n## Questions\n\n1. Does a prudent agent exist? If so, give one of the smallest possible modal depth, with its outcome table against\n   CooperateBot, DefectBot, FairBot and itself.\n2. Prove that no agent of smaller modal depth is prudent, for formulas of any size.\n3. If you can: characterize the prudent agents of that minimal depth, or bound their minimal size.\n\n## Checker\n\n`/work/check.py` is a reference implementation of exactly the semantics above. Run\n`python3 check.py \"<formula>\"`; it prints the formula's modal depth, its actions against CooperateBot, DefectBot,\nFairBot and itself, FairBot's action against it, and whether it is prudent. Syntax: atoms `a`, `c`, `d`; constants `T`, `F`; `~`, `&`, `|`, `->`;\n`B(...)` for □. For example FairBot is `B(a)`.\n\n## Evidence\n\n- All 801 pairwise-inequivalent fully modalized formulas over a, c and d were enumerated, up to modal depth 2 and 7\n  symbols and without constants, each combined with either default. None is prudent.\n- Every depth-1 agent whose formula is a Boolean combination of at most two boxes, each box over any Boolean function\n  of a, c and d (constants included): 523,264 agents, none prudent.\n- A random sample of 400,000 such agents with three boxes: none prudent.\n\n## Success and stopping\n\nYou have succeeded, and should stop, when all of the following hold:\n1. `/work/answer.md` contains a line `FORMULA: <formula>` with a formula that `check.py` reports as prudent, and a\n   line `DEPTH: <d>` giving its modal depth.\n2. `/work/answer.md` contains a complete proof that **no** agent of modal depth less than d is prudent (for formulas\n   of any size). Alternatively, if you find a prudent agent of smaller depth than any you can rule out, report it.\n3. You have written the file `/work/SUBMIT` (any content). Writing `SUBMIT` ends the attempt immediately for everyone\n   working on it, so write it only when 1 and 2 are done.\n\nIf time runs out before you submit, whatever is in `answer.md` at that moment is graded.\n\n## Deliverable\n\n`/work/answer.md` as described above, with any code you used in `/work`. Mark partial results clearly.\n----","stderr":"","interrupted":false,"isImage":false,"noOutputExpected":false},"tool_result_meta":[{"id":"toolu_015uNz3CzWSeK1WzpuofVrQF","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}]}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-sonnet-5-5","id":"msg_011CgJVQnbxsJWbgXdcD5ywa","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01RSDo2ZMFgMpqexBYCTfS4E","name":"Bash","input":{"command":"cd /work; cat check.py; ls"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1618,"cache_read_input_tokens":16358,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":1618,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":20,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"a3c97900-83d0-4a07-b953-bbca2955c090","uuid":"13685062-7fb7-4e1c-a4a4-13fa8e859982","timestamp":"2026-10-23T03:52:33.819Z","request_id":"req_011CgJVQnFNbQ9Pydkjzvw3X"}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01RSDo2ZMFgMpqexBYCTfS4E","type":"tool_result","content":"#!/usr/bin/env python3\n\"\"\"Reference checker for the prudent-modal-agents problem: the problem's own semantics in code.\n\nUsage:  python3 check.py \"B(a) & ~B(c)\"\n\nSyntax: atoms a, c, d (about the current opponent); constants T, F; ~ (not), & (and), | (or), -> (implies);\nB(...) is the box (PA proves). Every atom must be inside some B(...). Precedence: ~ binds tightest, then &, then |,\nthen -> (right-associative); use parentheses freely.\n\nPrints the formula's modal depth, its stabilized actions against CooperateBot (T), DefectBot (F), FairBot (B(a)) and\nitself, FairBot's action against it, and whether it is prudent.\n\"\"\"\nimport sys, re\nfrom functools import lru_cache\nsys.setrecursionlimit(100000)\nTOK = re.compile(r\"\\s*(->|[()~&|]|B|[acdTF])\")\ndef tokenize(s):\n    out, i = [], 0\n    s = s.strip()\n    while i < len(s):\n        m = TOK.match(s, i)\n        if not m: raise SyntaxError(f\"bad character at {i}: {s[i:i+10]!r}\")\n        out.append(m.group(1)); i = m.end()\n        while i < len(s) and s[i].isspace(): i += 1\n    return out\nclass P:\n    def __init__(self, toks): self.t, self.i = toks, 0\n    def peek(self): return self.t[self.i] if self.i < len(self.t) else None\n    def eat(self, x=None):\n        tok = self.peek()\n        if x and tok != x: raise SyntaxError(f\"expected {x}, got {tok}\")\n        self.i += 1; return tok\n    def imp(self):\n        l = self.orr()\n        if self.peek() == \"->\": self.eat(); return (\"or\", (\"not\", l), self.imp())\n        return l\n    def orr(self):\n        l = self.andd()\n        while self.peek() == \"|\": self.eat(); l = (\"or\", l, self.andd())\n        return l\n    def andd(self):\n        l = self.un()\n        while self.peek() == \"&\": self.eat(); l = (\"and\", l, self.un())\n        return l\n    def un(self):\n        tok = self.peek()\n        if tok == \"~\": self.eat(); return (\"not\", self.un())\n        if tok == \"B\": self.eat(); self.eat(\"(\"); f = self.imp(); self.eat(\")\"); return (\"box\", f)\n        if tok == \"(\": self.eat(); f = self.imp(); self.eat(\")\"); return f\n        if tok in (\"a\", \"c\", \"d\", \"T\", \"F\"): self.eat(); return (tok,)\n        raise SyntaxError(f\"unexpected {tok}\")\ndef parse(s):\n    p = P(tokenize(s)); f = p.imp()\n    if p.peek() is not None: raise SyntaxError(f\"trailing input at token {p.i}\")\n    return f\ndef depth(f):\n    if f[0] == \"box\": return 1 + depth(f[1])\n    return max([depth(g) for g in f[1:]], default=0)\ndef modalized(f, inside=False):\n    if f[0] in (\"a\", \"c\", \"d\"): return inside\n    if f[0] == \"box\": return modalized(f[1], True)\n    return all(modalized(g, inside) for g in f[1:])\ndef outcomes(formula_text, worlds=None):\n    X = parse(formula_text)\n    if not modalized(X): raise ValueError(\"not fully modalized: every atom must be inside B(...)\")\n    agents = {\"CB\": (\"T\",), \"DB\": (\"F\",), \"FB\": (\"box\", (\"a\",)), \"X\": X}\n    W = worlds or (6 * (depth(X) + 2) + 10)\n    @lru_cache(maxsize=None)\n    def act(x, y, n):                      # does agent x cooperate with agent y, at world n?\n        return ev(agents[x], n, x, y)\n    def ev(f, n, me, opp):\n        t = f[0]\n        if t == \"T\": return True\n        if t == \"F\": return False\n        if t == \"a\": return act(opp, me, n)\n        if t == \"c\": return act(opp, \"CB\", n)\n        if t == \"d\": return act(opp, \"DB\", n)\n        if t == \"not\": return not ev(f[1], n, me, opp)\n        if t == \"and\": return ev(f[1], n, me, opp) and ev(f[2], n, me, opp)\n        if t == \"or\": return ev(f[1], n, me, opp) or ev(f[2], n, me, opp)\n        if t == \"box\": return all(ev(f[1], m, me, opp) for m in range(n))\n    res = {}\n    for key, (x, y) in {\"CB\": (\"X\", \"CB\"), \"DB\": (\"X\", \"DB\"), \"FB\": (\"X\", \"FB\"), \"X\": (\"X\", \"X\"),\n                        \"FB_reply\": (\"FB\", \"X\")}.items():\n        seq = [act(x, y, n) for n in range(W)]\n        tail = seq[W // 2:]\n        if len(set(tail)) != 1: raise RuntimeError(f\"no stable value for {x} against {y} within {W} worlds\")\n        res[key] = \"C\" if tail[-1] else \"D\"\n    return depth(X), res\nif __name__ == \"__main__\":\n    if len(sys.argv) != 2: print(__doc__); sys.exit(2)\n    d, r = outcomes(sys.argv[1])\n    prudent = r[\"CB\"] == \"D\" and r[\"DB\"] == \"D\" and r[\"FB\"] == \"C\" and r[\"FB_reply\"] == \"C\" and r[\"X\"] == \"C\"\n    print(f\"depth {d}; vs CooperateBot {r['CB']}, vs DefectBot {r['DB']}, vs FairBot {r['FB']} (FairBot replies \"\n          f\"{r['FB_reply']}), vs itself {r['X']}; prudent: {'YES' if prudent else 'no'}\")\nboard.md\ncheck.py\nlogs\nproblem.md","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"a3c97900-83d0-4a07-b953-bbca2955c090","uuid":"b45739ac-3338-4513-8e08-83f78b1d22df","timestamp":"2026-10-23T03:52:33.836Z","tool_use_result":{"stdout":"#!/usr/bin/env python3\n\"\"\"Reference checker for the prudent-modal-agents problem: the problem's own semantics in code.\n\nUsage:  python3 check.py \"B(a) & ~B(c)\"\n\nSyntax: atoms a, c, d (about the current opponent); constants T, F; ~ (not), & (and), | (or), -> (implies);\nB(...) is the box (PA proves). Every atom must be inside some B(...). Precedence: ~ binds tightest, then &, then |,\nthen -> (right-associative); use parentheses freely.\n\nPrints the formula's modal depth, its stabilized actions against CooperateBot (T), DefectBot (F), FairBot (B(a)) and\nitself, FairBot's action against it, and whether it is prudent.\n\"\"\"\nimport sys, re\nfrom functools import lru_cache\nsys.setrecursionlimit(100000)\nTOK = re.compile(r\"\\s*(->|[()~&|]|B|[acdTF])\")\ndef tokenize(s):\n    out, i = [], 0\n    s = s.strip()\n    while i < len(s):\n        m = TOK.match(s, i)\n        if not m: raise SyntaxError(f\"bad character at {i}: {s[i:i+10]!r}\")\n        out.append(m.group(1)); i = m.end()\n        while i < len(s) and s[i].isspace(): i += 1\n    return out\nclass P:\n    def __init__(self, toks): self.t, self.i = toks, 0\n    def peek(self): return self.t[self.i] if self.i < len(self.t) else None\n    def eat(self, x=None):\n        tok = self.peek()\n        if x and tok != x: raise SyntaxError(f\"expected {x}, got {tok}\")\n        self.i += 1; return tok\n    def imp(self):\n        l = self.orr()\n        if self.peek() == \"->\": self.eat(); return (\"or\", (\"not\", l), self.imp())\n        return l\n    def orr(self):\n        l = self.andd()\n        while self.peek() == \"|\": self.eat(); l = (\"or\", l, self.andd())\n        return l\n    def andd(self):\n        l = self.un()\n        while self.peek() == \"&\": self.eat(); l = (\"and\", l, self.un())\n        return l\n    def un(self):\n        tok = self.peek()\n        if tok == \"~\": self.eat(); return (\"not\", self.un())\n        if tok == \"B\": self.eat(); self.eat(\"(\"); f = self.imp(); self.eat(\")\"); return (\"box\", f)\n        if tok == \"(\": self.eat(); f = self.imp(); self.eat(\")\"); return f\n        if tok in (\"a\", \"c\", \"d\", \"T\", \"F\"): self.eat(); return (tok,)\n        raise SyntaxError(f\"unexpected {tok}\")\ndef parse(s):\n    p = P(tokenize(s)); f = p.imp()\n    if p.peek() is not None: raise SyntaxError(f\"trailing input at token {p.i}\")\n    return f\ndef depth(f):\n    if f[0] == \"box\": return 1 + depth(f[1])\n    return max([depth(g) for g in f[1:]], default=0)\ndef modalized(f, inside=False):\n    if f[0] in (\"a\", \"c\", \"d\"): return inside\n    if f[0] == \"box\": return modalized(f[1], True)\n    return all(modalized(g, inside) for g in f[1:])\ndef outcomes(formula_text, worlds=None):\n    X = parse(formula_text)\n    if not modalized(X): raise ValueError(\"not fully modalized: every atom must be inside B(...)\")\n    agents = {\"CB\": (\"T\",), \"DB\": (\"F\",), \"FB\": (\"box\", (\"a\",)), \"X\": X}\n    W = worlds or (6 * (depth(X) + 2) + 10)\n    @lru_cache(maxsize=None)\n    def act(x, y, n):                      # does agent x cooperate with agent y, at world n?\n        return ev(agents[x], n, x, y)\n    def ev(f, n, me, opp):\n        t = f[0]\n        if t == \"T\": return True\n        if t == \"F\": return False\n        if t == \"a\": return act(opp, me, n)\n        if t == \"c\": return act(opp, \"CB\", n)\n        if t == \"d\": return act(opp, \"DB\", n)\n        if t == \"not\": return not ev(f[1], n, me, opp)\n        if t == \"and\": return ev(f[1], n, me, opp) and ev(f[2], n, me, opp)\n        if t == \"or\": return ev(f[1], n, me, opp) or ev(f[2], n, me, opp)\n        if t == \"box\": return all(ev(f[1], m, me, opp) for m in range(n))\n    res = {}\n    for key, (x, y) in {\"CB\": (\"X\", \"CB\"), \"DB\": (\"X\", \"DB\"), \"FB\": (\"X\", \"FB\"), \"X\": (\"X\", \"X\"),\n                        \"FB_reply\": (\"FB\", \"X\")}.items():\n        seq = [act(x, y, n) for n in range(W)]\n        tail = seq[W // 2:]\n        if len(set(tail)) != 1: raise RuntimeError(f\"no stable value for {x} against {y} within {W} worlds\")\n        res[key] = \"C\" if tail[-1] else \"D\"\n    return depth(X), res\nif __name__ == \"__main__\":\n    if len(sys.argv) != 2: print(__doc__); sys.exit(2)\n    d, r = outcomes(sys.argv[1])\n    prudent = r[\"CB\"] == \"D\" and r[\"DB\"] == \"D\" and r[\"FB\"] == \"C\" and r[\"FB_reply\"] == \"C\" and r[\"X\"] == \"C\"\n    print(f\"depth {d}; vs CooperateBot {r['CB']}, vs DefectBot {r['DB']}, vs FairBot {r['FB']} (FairBot replies \"\n          f\"{r['FB_reply']}), vs itself {r['X']}; prudent: {'YES' if prudent else 'no'}\")\nboard.md\ncheck.py\nlogs\nproblem.md","stderr":"","interrupted":false,"isImage":false,"noOutputExpected":false},"tool_result_meta":[{"id":"toolu_01RSDo2ZMFgMpqexBYCTfS4E","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}]}
